    question_en: "A healthcare records startup runs a mission-critical electronic health record (EHR) analytics platform on Amazon EC2 instances in the us-east-1 Region. The application's database runs on an EC2 instance in Availability Zone us-east-1a with an attached 1 TB Amazon EBS General Purpose SSD (gp3) volume. To satisfy disaster recovery (DR) compliance mandates, the startup maintains daily EBS snapshots of the volume and has an automated runbook to launch a recovery EC2 instance in Availability Zone us-east-1b during an outage.\n\nDuring DR simulation testing, the engineering team creates a new EBS volume from the latest snapshot in us-east-1b and attaches it to the recovery instance. Although the instance boots successfully, database read operations experience acute I/O latency spikes and severe throughput degradation for the first two hours. Analysis confirms this is caused by storage blocks being lazy-loaded from Amazon S3 upon first access.\n\nWhich solution should a solutions architect recommend to eliminate this initial latency penalty and ensure the restored volume delivers full provisioned performance immediately in us-east-1b?",
