    explanation_en: "**Correct Answer: Option 3 (Configure a bucket policy on the Amazon S3 bucket that allows read actions and uses the aws:PrincipalOrgID condition key set to the organization ID.)**\n\n- **Why it is correct:** The `aws:PrincipalOrgID` global condition key in an Amazon S3 bucket policy allows access to be restricted to all IAM principals from accounts within a specific AWS Organizations organization. This eliminates the need to explicitly list or manage individual account IDs. When new accounts are added to the organization as the airline expands, they automatically inherit access permissions without requiring any updates to the bucket policy, achieving the goal with the least operational overhead.\n- **Why Option 1 is incorrect:** Service Control Policies (SCPs) define the maximum permission boundaries (guardrails) for accounts or OUs in an organization, but they *never* grant permissions. Effective permissions require an IAM policy or resource-based policy grant. Furthermore, an SCP cannot grant cross-account access to a resource residing in another account.\n- **Why Option 2 is incorrect:** AWS Resource Access Manager (AWS RAM) enables sharing of supported AWS resources (such as VPC subnets, Transit Gateways, and Route 53 Resolver rules) across accounts, but Amazon S3 buckets are not supported by AWS RAM. Cross-account S3 access must be managed using S3 bucket policies, IAM policies, or S3 Access Points.\n- **Why Option 4 is incorrect:** Listing each account ID in the `Principal` element of the bucket policy grants cross-account access, but it requires continuous manual or automated modifications every time a new account is provisioned. This introduces substantial operational overhead and risks exceeding the 20 KB maximum limit for S3 bucket policies."
